on-arrival: ko_merger_stage = 1; ko_coalition_dissent += 3; ko_consolidation_outgoing_leaders = "Donald Tusk (PO), Adam Szłapka (Nowoczesna) and Barbara Nowacka (Inicjatywa Polska) as leaders of separate organisations"; ko_consolidation_incoming_bloc = "Donald Tusk backed by PO's dominant apparatus and pro-merger majorities in Nowoczesna and Inicjatywa Polska"; ko_leader = "Donald Tusk"; news_headline = "KO's component parties open a six-part consolidation negotiation"

= October 2025 — A coalition is not yet one party

**Outgoing → incoming:** Donald Tusk's PO, Adam Szłapka's Nowoczesna and
Barbara Nowacka's Inicjatywa Polska enter as separately led organisations. The
historical consolidation creates one KO party under Tusk; PO's dominant
apparatus and pro-merger majorities in the two smaller parties support him,
while the Greens remain a separate ally.

**Why now:** years of campaigning under one coalition label have made three
memberships, structures, debts and sets of candidate rules an electoral
liability. The convention negotiates the terms, representation and treatment
of dissenters around the consolidation.

For Lewica, one disciplined liberal competitor is harder to out-organise, but
the programme, local-list and minority disputes create openings. Lewica can
condition cooperation, compete for organisers, defend a social-investment
floor or attack the apparatus deal; it cannot cast another party's ballot or
appoint its leader.

- @ko_merger_leadership

